WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.779
You run the tests with Gradle.

2
00:00:04.779 --> 00:00:06.096
You change nothing.

3
00:00:06.096 --> 00:00:07.413
You run them again.

4
00:00:07.633 --> 00:00:09.691
Does Gradle run every test again?

5
00:00:09.691 --> 00:00:13.620
And if it doesn't, how would it even know that nothing changed?

6
00:00:14.433 --> 00:00:16.955
First, why Kotlin needs a build at all.

7
00:00:16.955 --> 00:00:19.864
Python runs straight from the text you write.

8
00:00:19.864 --> 00:00:24.907
Kotlin source must be compiled, translated into class files a machine can run.

9
00:00:25.133 --> 00:00:29.125
Then those get packed into a jar, one zip file of compiled code.

10
00:00:29.125 --> 00:00:33.865
Add tests and libraries, and that's a lot of steps to do in the right order.

11
00:00:33.865 --> 00:00:36.609
That's the job of a build tool, like Gradle.

12
00:00:37.433 --> 00:00:41.940
Now a bakery, the night before a wedding order of two hundred sandwiches.

13
00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.767
A new baker takes the list, starts at the top, and works down.

14
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.134
At midnight, the bread isn't ready.

15
00:00:48.134 --> 00:00:52.157
Nobody started the dough first, and the starter alone takes hours.

16
00:00:52.367 --> 00:00:54.251
He followed the list perfectly.

17
00:00:54.251 --> 00:00:55.528
That was the problem.

18
00:00:55.528 --> 00:01:00.088
It followed the order of the menu, not the order the work has to happen in.

19
00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.349
A real kitchen plans backwards from what is needed.

20
00:01:03.349 --> 00:01:07.713
And it never makes a sauce twice when this morning's batch is still good.

21
00:01:07.713 --> 00:01:09.685
That's exactly how Gradle thinks.

22
00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:17.839
In Gradle, each line of the prep list is a task: a named piece of work, like compileKotlin, test, or jar.

23
00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:19.252
You ask for build.

24
00:01:19.252 --> 00:01:22.283
Gradle starts from there, and plans backwards.

25
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.087
Build does no work itself.

26
00:01:24.087 --> 00:01:28.785
It groups assemble, which makes the outputs, and check, which runs the tests.

27
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:30.674
Assemble needs the jar.

28
00:01:30.674 --> 00:01:32.930
The jar needs compiled classes.

29
00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:35.185
The classes need compileKotlin.

30
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:41.593
And check needs test, which needs the compiled tests, which need the main code compiled first.

31
00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:45.659
Notice compileKotlin is needed twice, but it appears once.

32
00:01:45.659 --> 00:01:49.984
Gradle runs each task once, in an order that respects the arrows.

33
00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:53.876
Now the cooking goes forwards, from the starter to the sandwiches.

34
00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:55.858
This plan is the task graph.

35
00:01:55.858 --> 00:01:58.746
Its arrows point one way, and never loop back.

36
00:01:58.746 --> 00:02:01.885
The baker's prep list, in the right order at last.

37
00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.155
Now the second half of the wish.

38
00:02:04.155 --> 00:02:05.889
Never make the sauce twice.

39
00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:09.119
Every task declares its inputs and its outputs.

40
00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:15.991
compileKotlin reads source files and the classpath, the list of jars it can see, and it writes class files.

41
00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.210
Before a task runs, Gradle fingerprints them.

42
00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:28.373
It hashes their contents, where one changed byte changes the hash, and compares with the hashes it saved in the .gradle folder last time.

43
00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:31.557
So: run the build again, changing nothing.

44
00:02:31.557 --> 00:02:32.684
Pause and guess.

45
00:02:32.684 --> 00:02:34.444
Which tasks do real work?

46
00:02:36.667 --> 00:02:37.490
None of them.

47
00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:40.338
Every task prints UP-TO-DATE, and is skipped.

48
00:02:40.338 --> 00:02:42.553
This morning's sauce is still good.

49
00:02:43.267 --> 00:02:44.686
Let's try to fool it.

50
00:02:44.686 --> 00:02:48.876
touch updates a file's time, but not one byte of its contents.

51
00:02:48.876 --> 00:02:50.497
Does Gradle fall for it?

52
00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.016
All seven tasks stay up to date.

53
00:02:53.016 --> 00:02:55.983
Gradle compares contents, not timestamps.

54
00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.016
Now change one test file.

55
00:02:58.016 --> 00:02:59.904
Watch which tasks wake up.

56
00:03:00.133 --> 00:03:02.229
compileTestKotlin and test run.

57
00:03:02.229 --> 00:03:08.381
compileKotlin and jar stay up to date, because their inputs are the main source files only.

58
00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:10.673
One question is left.

59
00:03:10.673 --> 00:03:14.923
How can Gradle know the whole graph before it runs a single task?

60
00:03:14.923 --> 00:03:16.884
It writes the prep list first.

61
00:03:16.884 --> 00:03:18.649
Every run has three phases.

62
00:03:18.867 --> 00:03:21.530
Initialization reads the settings file.

63
00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:26.924
Configuration runs the build scripts, which register tasks and build the graph.

64
00:03:26.924 --> 00:03:29.383
Only then, execution runs the tasks.

65
00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:31.612
Here's a test from the lesson.

66
00:03:31.612 --> 00:03:40.600
Four print lines: one in settings, one at the top of the build script, one inside the hello task's setup block, and one inside doLast.

67
00:03:40.833 --> 00:03:42.341
You run the hello task.

68
00:03:42.341 --> 00:03:45.881
Pause and guess: which lines print, and in what order?

69
00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:48.699
All four.

70
00:03:48.699 --> 00:03:54.090
One, two and two b print before any task starts, while the plan is being written.

71
00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:56.952
Only line three comes from the task itself.

72
00:03:57.167 --> 00:04:01.100
A build script looks like a program that does the build.

73
00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:01.732
It isn't.

74
00:04:01.732 --> 00:04:03.628
It only describes the work.

75
00:04:03.628 --> 00:04:07.561
Only the code inside doLast runs when the task executes.

76
00:04:07.767 --> 00:04:09.100
Run it a second time.

77
00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:16.463
The configuration cache saved the finished prep list, so phases one and two are skipped, and only line three prints.

78
00:04:17.367 --> 00:04:20.211
A few guardrails follow straight from this picture.

79
00:04:20.433 --> 00:04:27.266
Top-level code in a build script runs every time Gradle configures the build, even for gradlew help.

80
00:04:27.266 --> 00:04:30.751
So keep network calls and big file reads out of it.

81
00:04:30.967 --> 00:04:36.008
A task with no declared outputs always runs, because there's nothing to compare.

82
00:04:36.233 --> 00:04:41.924
If you often reach for rerun tasks to force work, a task is probably missing an input.

83
00:04:41.924 --> 00:04:43.777
Fix the build logic instead.

84
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.115
And always run ./gradlew, the Gradle Wrapper.

85
00:04:47.115 --> 00:04:53.208
It pins the exact Gradle version, so your laptop and every CI runner build the same way.

86
00:04:54.133 --> 00:04:55.036
So, the moral.

87
00:04:55.036 --> 00:05:03.939
Gradle plans backwards from what you asked for, cooks forwards in dependency order, and skips every task whose fingerprints didn't change.

88
00:05:04.167 --> 00:05:07.352
The baker who finally reads the list from the bottom.

89
00:05:07.567 --> 00:05:08.507
Quick check.

90
00:05:08.507 --> 00:05:12.345
A print line sits at the top of build.gradle.kts.

91
00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:14.617
In which phase does it print?

92
00:05:14.617 --> 00:05:15.871
Pause and think.

93
00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:19.027
Configuration.

94
00:05:19.027 --> 00:05:22.999
It prints while the plan is written, before any task starts.
